-#
-#  The COLOURS of the four Moss kinds (Fin::MossKinds) and the small marks that
-#  go with them: the kind chip (Fin::MossTransactionsHelper#moss_kind_chip), the
-#  coloured rail on a wallet row, the kind tiles of /fin/moss, the status tags
-#  and the green of an incoming amount. Ruby knows the icon and the class name,
-#  this file knows what they look like -- no colour value exists twice.
-#
-#  EVERY view that shows a chip, a rail, a tile or a tag must render it:
-#      = render "shared/wsjrdp/moss_kind_styles"
-#  The guard emits the <style> only once per request, so rendering it from a
-#  page AND from a partial it embeds is harmless (same pattern as
-#  shared/wsjrdp/_expandable_table_styles).
-#
-#  hitobito has no dark theme, so the light palette below is the whole story.
-#  The four hues (blue, ochre, violet, blue-green) stay apart under the common
-#  colour-vision deficiencies; green is reserved for money coming in and red for
-#  returns and warnings, so neither marks a kind.
- return if @wsjrdp_moss_kind_styles_emitted
- @wsjrdp_moss_kind_styles_emitted = true
:css
  /* The only place the palette is written down: each kind class carries its two
     colours as custom properties, everything below reads them. --moss-kind-fg
     is the text/line colour, --moss-kind-bg the pale fill of the chip. The
     class itself (moss-kind-<expense_type>) comes from Fin::MossKinds. */
  .moss-kind-card_transaction { --moss-kind-fg: #1d5cb0; --moss-kind-bg: #e2ecfa; }
  .moss-kind-invoice          { --moss-kind-fg: #a66a0c; --moss-kind-bg: #fbecd2; }
  .moss-kind-reimbursement    { --moss-kind-fg: #6b4aa6; --moss-kind-bg: #ebe4f7; }
  .moss-kind-top_up           { --moss-kind-fg: #166f5c; --moss-kind-bg: #dcefe8; }

  /* The chip: a small pill of icon + word. The gap keeps the two apart, the
     transparent border keeps the height stable in forced-colors mode (where the
     background disappears and only the outline remains). */
  .moss-kind {
    display: inline-flex; align-items: center; gap: .35rem;
    padding: .12rem .5rem .12rem .38rem; border: 1px solid transparent;
    border-radius: 999px; white-space: nowrap;
    font-size: .76rem; font-weight: 500; line-height: 1.5;
    color: var(--moss-kind-fg); background: var(--moss-kind-bg);
  }
  .moss-kind .fas { font-size: .85em; }

  /* The rail: a 3px line inside the row's first cell, so the kind stays
     readable while scrolling even when the chip has scrolled out of view. It
     is painted in the chip's pale FILL colour (--moss-kind-bg), not the text
     colour, so a page of rows reads as a quiet margin rather than a stack of
     bars. An inset box-shadow, not a border -- a border would move the cell's
     content and fight the table's own borders. Bootstrap 5.2 paints a row's
     hover and stripe tint through an inset box-shadow of its own
     (--bs-table-accent-bg) on every cell; that one is repeated here, or the
     first cell would lose the tint the rest of the row gets. The cell's own
     padding grows by the rail's width, so the content keeps its distance from
     the rail the same as from the cell edge.
     A row carries the class where one row is one record (the wallet); where a
     record owns a GROUP of rows -- the transactions list, whose reimbursement
     brings an expense row per expense -- the group's tbody carries it, and the
     rail then runs along every row of the group. */
  tr.moss-kind-card_transaction > td:first-child,
  tr.moss-kind-invoice > td:first-child,
  tr.moss-kind-reimbursement > td:first-child,
  tr.moss-kind-top_up > td:first-child,
  tbody.moss-kind-card_transaction > tr > td:first-child,
  tbody.moss-kind-invoice > tr > td:first-child,
  tbody.moss-kind-reimbursement > tr > td:first-child,
  tbody.moss-kind-top_up > tr > td:first-child {
    box-shadow: inset 3px 0 0 var(--moss-kind-bg), inset 0 0 0 9999px var(--bs-table-accent-bg, transparent);
    padding-left: 7px; /* the table's 4px cell padding plus the 3px rail */
  }

  /* The overview tiles (/fin/moss): the kind's colour as a top edge. The
     fallback keeps a tile without a kind class (the total tile) unmarked
     instead of borderless-but-shifted. !important because the tiles also wear
     Bootstrap's .border utility, which declares the whole border !important
     and would otherwise paint every edge grey. */
  .moss-kind-tile { border-top: 3px solid var(--moss-kind-fg, transparent) !important; }

  /* Status tags after the first line of a row (foreign currency, Gutschrift,
     a deviating status): outline only, no fill -- they qualify a row, they do
     not classify it, so they must not compete with the chip. This Bootstrap
     build has no --bs-secondary-color (a 5.3 token), hence the concrete
     fallbacks. */
  .moss-tag {
    display: inline-block; margin-left: .4rem; padding: 0 .4rem;
    font-size: .7rem; line-height: 1.5; white-space: nowrap; vertical-align: 1px;
    border: 1px solid var(--bs-border-color, #ced4da); border-radius: 3px;
    background: transparent; color: var(--bs-secondary-color, #6c757d);
  }

  /* Money coming IN (a top-up, the rare card credit note): green, and the "+"
     sign is rendered next to it -- again, colour is never the only cue. */
  .moss-amount-in { color: #1e7b3d; }

  /* A filter preset that stands for one kind (Wsjrdp::TableState::FilterPreset
     with icon + css_class): the toggle link keeps its plain button shape, only
     its icon is tinted, and switching it on fills it with the kind's pale
     background. The :hover variants are spelled out because the builder's own
     [aria-pressed="true"]:hover rule has the same specificity as the plain one
     here and would otherwise win by source order. */
  .flt-preset.moss-kind-card_transaction .fas,
  .flt-preset.moss-kind-invoice .fas,
  .flt-preset.moss-kind-reimbursement .fas,
  .flt-preset.moss-kind-top_up .fas { color: var(--moss-kind-fg); }
  /* ... except the on/off tick, which is a filled dot with a white check of its
     own. Same specificity as the rule above, so it has to come after it. */
  .flt-preset .flt-preset-tick .fas { color: inherit; }
  .flt-preset.moss-kind-card_transaction[aria-pressed="true"],
  .flt-preset.moss-kind-invoice[aria-pressed="true"],
  .flt-preset.moss-kind-reimbursement[aria-pressed="true"],
  .flt-preset.moss-kind-top_up[aria-pressed="true"],
  .flt-preset.moss-kind-card_transaction[aria-pressed="true"]:hover,
  .flt-preset.moss-kind-invoice[aria-pressed="true"]:hover,
  .flt-preset.moss-kind-reimbursement[aria-pressed="true"]:hover,
  .flt-preset.moss-kind-top_up[aria-pressed="true"]:hover {
    background: var(--moss-kind-bg); border-color: var(--moss-kind-fg);
  }
